course: dictionary
lesson: 13035
title: Familiarity: knowing something well
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Familiarity means knowing something well because you have seen it, heard it or done it many times. «Her familiarity with the rules helped her win the game.» She has played so often that the rules feel easy to her.
explain: Familiarity can also mean a friendly, relaxed way of behaving, as if you know someone well. «The cat rubbed against Sam's legs with easy familiarity.» The cat acts as if Sam is an old friend.
explain: Be careful with the word when it is used in a formal way. «He called the head teacher "mate" and put his arm round her, which was too much familiarity for someone he had only just met.» Here familiarity means behaving too casually, and that is rude.
fact: Familiarity comes from the word familiar, which means well known to you.
fact: «Familiarity with» is the phrase to use when you know something well, such as «familiarity with times tables».
fact: «A sense of familiarity» is the feeling that you have been somewhere or seen something before.
fact: «Familiarity breeds contempt» is a saying. It means that when you know something too well, you may stop respecting it.
fact: Too much familiarity with someone you do not know well is rude, so be polite and wait until you know them better.
exercise: «Her familiarity with the rules helped her win the game.» What does familiarity with the rules mean? | *Knowing them well from practice | Reading them only once | Forgetting them each week | Breaking them on purpose
exercise: «The cat rubbed against Sam's legs with easy familiarity, as if they were old friends.» What does familiarity mean here? | *A relaxed, friendly way, as if they know each other well | Being very angry with someone | Not noticing someone at all | Being scared of someone new
exercise: «Walking into my bedroom gave me a sense of familiarity, because everything was where I left it.» What does a sense of familiarity feel like? | *Knowing a place well and feeling at home | Being very hungry | Feeling lost in a strange town | Hearing a loud noise
test: «He called the head teacher "mate" and put his arm round her, which was too much familiarity for someone he had only just met.» What does too much familiarity mean? | Knowing a teacher very well from many lessons | Speaking very quietly | *Behaving too casually or rudely with someone you do not know well | Being shy with new people
test: The saying «Familiarity breeds contempt» means what? | Things you know well are always boring to learn | Nothing you know well can ever be useful | *When you know something too well, you may stop respecting it | Friends always become enemies in time
exam: «Her familiarity with times tables helps her answer quickly.» What does familiarity with times tables mean? | *Knowing them well from lots of practice | Forgetting the answers each day | Hating maths lessons | Writing the tables in capital letters
